 "transcript": {
  "answer": "The RBA's cash rate target is **4.60%**. It has been at that level since **30 September 2026**, and the latest daily data I have is for 15 October 2026. That's the highest it has been in this cycle.\n\n**How it got there**\n\nAfter cutting through 2025, the RBA has raised rates four times in 2026, each by 0.25 points:\n\n| Date it took effect | Cash rate target |\n|---|---|\n| 2025 (cuts) | 4.35% → 3.60% (lowest point, held from Sep 2025 to Jan 2026) |\n| 4 Feb 2026 | 3.85% |\n| 18 Mar 2026 | 4.10% |\n| 6 May 2026 | 4.35% |\n| 30 Sep 2026 | **4.60%** |\n\nSo rates are up 1 full point since January. The RBA has reversed all of its 2025 cuts and gone 0.25 points higher than the 4.35% peak of the last cycle. It held at 4.35% from May to September before the latest rise.\n\n**One data note:** the RBA's daily rate series shows the move to 4.60% on 30 September. However, its separate list of announced changes doesn't include that hike yet and ends at the May increase. The daily figure is the more direct measure, but this rise has the least confirmation in the data.\n\n**Why it's raising rates: inflation is high and going up**\n- **Headline CPI:** 4.0% over the year to August 2026. It was 3.5% in July and peaked at 4.6% in March.\n- **Trimmed mean** (the RBA's preferred measure of underlying inflation): 3.6% in August 2026, up from 3.2% in September 2025. It has climbed steadily and has stayed well above the RBA's 2–3% target band.\n\nBecause underlying inflation is still rising rather than easing, the RBA is clearly still focused on tightening.",
